The test was a 24x28 repeating 3x3 panel field separated by color-9 bands, with distinctive colored values at selected separator intersections. Two demos confirmed that this general puzzle class can produce compact outputs, but neither established a test mapping: demo 1 was 23x29 -> 5x4, demo 2 was 26x29 -> 3x4, while the test was 24x28. Their compact outputs preserve some colors from the separator markings and include the filler/background value (9 in demo 1, 7 in demo 2). Dimensions do not match a simple count of all intersections or panels. The tested agent requested a third demo without stating a concrete pair of competing mappings or the predicted observation that would distinguish them; no verified output rule was obtained.

Reusable diagnostic: before requesting another example, lay out candidate coordinate maps and output conventions. For each demo, record exact positions/values of exceptional separator cells and compact output. Test possible selection of intersections, row/column reduction, edge inclusion, ordering, and background/default encoding against every observed pair. Make a further request only if the competing hypotheses yield a specific different predicted outcome, then immediately use the returned example to resolve that difference. Do not infer the test result from visual lattice resemblance or output shape alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-compact-panel-encoding/references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md"}

