{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md", "content": "# Compact-output diagnostic: distinguish tile summaries from noise

In a fresh Continual-ARC instance, the test was a 22×27 partitioned canvas with compact separator-defined regions, but six requested demos had unrelated palettes and dimensions, each yielding compact outputs (examples included 3×4, 3×4, 2×6, 6×2, 5×4, and 1×1). The demos may share the abstract representation of compressing a partitioned canvas, but no exact mapping from each demo's tile contents to output values was derived. Two speculative test submissions were wrong; subsequent requests did not resolve the transformation. This is a diagnostic, not a solved rule.

Reusable decision: prioritize output dimensions and potential partition-to-output mapping over salient test markings. For each demo, count tile rows/columns and map each candidate tile feature (marker color, marker presence, or pattern) to the corresponding output cell; test exact predictions across all pairs. If demonstrations vary, distinguish stable abstract layout from changing tile-level encoding instead of declaring them unrelated at a glance. Never infer test output from output dimensions alone. After a wrong submission, reopen representation and task class, and don't consume further demos without a named ambiguity and different predicted evidence. In any case, the user's interaction contract requires exactly one valid action JSON as the last line on every puzzle response; ensure even a process-limit summary contains a legal action, and do not claim the instance is resolved.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/marker-anchor-offsets.md", "content": "# Marker-to-template anchor offsets

When demos show sparse markers receiving copies of an embedded motif, keep two coordinate concepts separate: (1) the motif's bounding-box origin and (2) the motif coordinate that aligns with a marker. Represent the latter as a signed offset from the origin and validate it against multiple marker/copy pairs. A plausible colored pattern is not enough: replay every marker footprint against the complete output, including the original motif, overlaps, clipping, and unchanged candidate markers.

A repeated failure mode is to shift the right motif by an intuitively appealing amount (top-left, center, a nearby marker, or a visually salient source pixel) without validating the anchor. On incorrect feedback, discard that placement hypothesis and derive the offset anew from observed coordinate correspondences. Also validate serialization independently: every row must have the expected width and all values must be legal before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/marker-anchor-offsets.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u094/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md", "content": "# Compact-output rejection: reassess representation

A trial on a partitioned grid with 6×4 cells separated by full-width divider rows produced a 3×3 demo output. The initial response incorrectly submitted a speculative 5×5 grid, effectively disregarding the demonstrated output scale and region structure. The next action requested another demo without articulating a discriminating prediction. On future tasks, an output whose dimensions are smaller than the input should immediately trigger compact region mapping: locate separators, count regions, associate regions to output locations, and test marker presence/color/count/position against all observed outputs. After an incorrect submission, reopen task class and output representation before trying again. Request further demos only after naming competing mappings and the specific observation that will distinguish them.

